 class="matrx-pl-tag">video</span><span class="matrx-pl-tag">beginner</span><div class="matrx-pl-small">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;</div></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Articles</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong class="matrx-pl-link"><a href="https://iupac.org/what-we-do/periodic-table-of-elements/">IUPAC Periodic Table</a></strong><div>The official, authoritative version</div><div class="matrx-pl-muted matrx-pl-small">https://iupac.org/what-we-do/periodic-table-of-elements/</div></td><td><span class="matrx-pl-tag">article</span><span class="matrx-pl-tag">advanced</span><div class="matrx-pl-small">&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;&#9733;</div></td></tr></tbody></table></section>"`;

exports[`structured type layouts over real stored rows structured_info (md) a6e70a05-aeb6-4343-b21b-1048200dcb88 prints as its layout 1`] = `"<section class="matrx-pl"><h2 class="matrx-pl-title">Structured Information Extracted</h2><dl class="matrx-pl-kv"><dt>Total Elements</dt><dd>118 confirmed, 94 of them found in nature</dd><dt>Most Abundant (Universe)</dt><dd>Hydrogen, about 74% of normal matter</dd><dt>Densest Element</dt><dd>Osmium, roughly twice as dense as lead</dd><dt>Actionable Insight</dt><dd>Learn the groups first. Once you know an element&#39;s column, you can predict most of its behavior.</dd></dl></section>"`;

